{:edb/db-before-t 1211 :edb/db-after-t 1212 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534524 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:52:58.872Z" 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186177471 1078 #edb/ref 17592186177472 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186179719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424190 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186179967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186179967 1071 "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x, y)$?" 13194139534524 17592186045417 false] [17592186179967 1071 "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186179967 1074 "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)" 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186179973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177507 13194139534524 17592186045417 false] [17592186424183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424191 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186424183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424192 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186424183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424193 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186424183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424194 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186424183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424195 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [17592186424183 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534524 17592186045417 true] [175921864